.\" Hand-written: there is no parser to generate a file format from. The keys
.\" here must match mc_common::config — a tier-1 test asserts they do.
.TH MC-CONFIG 5 "" "mc" "mc manual"
.SH NAME
mc-config \- configuration file for the mc Minecraft server manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/minecraft/config.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
TOML, read by every
.BR mc (1)
command that needs to know how to run the server. A dpkg conffile: your edits
survive package upgrades, and dpkg asks before replacing it.
.PP
This file describes
.IR "how to run the server" .
What the server
.I is
\(em port, seed, MOTD, difficulty, game rules, RCON \(em lives in
.I /opt/minecraft/server.properties
instead, because the JVM reads and rewrites that file at every start. Nothing
from it is mirrored here; a copy could only go stale.
.PP
An unknown key is an error rather than a warning. A misspelled setting that
loaded silently would leave the server running on a default the operator
believes they changed.
.SH "[server]"
.TP
.B type
.RI ( vanilla ", " paper ", " fabric ", " neoforge ", default " vanilla )
Which upstream build to install. Anything else is refused at parse time.
.B forge
and
.B quilt
are named in the refusal rather than lumped in with typos: both have a real
upstream and are simply not supported yet.
.IP
Changing this after installation does not migrate a server. Install again with
.BR "mc install --type" ,
or restore into a fresh install.
.TP
.B version
.RI ( "string, default " latest )
The Minecraft version.
.B mc install
resolves
.B latest
and writes the concrete result back here, so a later
.B mc upgrade
can tell whether anything actually moved.
.IP
For
.B vanilla
and
.BR neoforge ,
an unchanged version means an unchanged artifact and
.B mc upgrade
skips the work. Paper publishes new builds against one Minecraft version and
Fabric ships new loader versions the same way, so for those two the upgrade
always runs.
.SH "[java]"
.TP
.B version
.RI ( integer ", unset by default )
Major Java version to launch with. Unset selects from the Minecraft version
being installed:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
1.20.5 and later, 1.21.x \(em Java 21
.IP \(bu 2
26.x \(em Java 25
.IP \(bu 2
1.18 to 1.20.4 \(em Java 17
.RE
.IP
Set it only to pin a runtime deliberately. If the pinned version is not
installed,
.B mc serve
warns and falls back to the system
.BR java ;
the GC flags below are then chosen from the runtime that actually starts, not
from the one this file asked for, because a Java 21 flag set handed to a Java
17 JVM is a boot failure.
.TP
.B ram
.RI ( "string, default " 4G )
Heap size, passed as both
.B \-Xmx
and
.BR \-Xms .
Equal minimum and maximum on purpose: a heap that grows under load pauses to do
it.
.TP
.B flags
.RI ( "list of strings, default empty" )
Garbage-collector flags. Empty auto-configures from the Java version \(em
generational ZGC on 21 and later, Aikar's G1GC tuning on 17. Setting this
replaces the preset entirely; it is not merged.
.TP
.B opts
.RI ( "list of strings, default empty" )
Extra JVM options, appended after the GC flags. For example
.RB [ \-Dfile.encoding=UTF-8 ].
.SH "[backup]"
Read by
.BR mc-backup (1),
which is a separate package. The keys are ignored if it is not installed.
.TP
.B keep
.RI ( "integer, default " 7 )
How many archives to retain. Rotation runs after a successful backup and
removes the oldest beyond this count.
.B 0
disables rotation \(em it does not delete everything.
.TP
.B schedule
.RI ( "string, default " daily )
systemd
.B OnCalendar=
syntax, for example
.BR weekly ,
or
.BR "Mon *-*-* 02:00:00" .
Takes effect at the next
.B mc install
or
.BR "mc upgrade" ,
which regenerates
.IR /etc/systemd/system/minecraft-backup.timer.d/schedule.conf .
See
.BR systemd.time (7).
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.RS
[server]
type = "paper"
version = "1.21.4"

[java]
ram = "8G"
opts = ["\-Dfile.encoding=UTF\-8"]

[backup]
keep = 14
schedule = "Mon *\-*\-* 02:00:00"
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/minecraft/config.toml
This file.
.TP
.I /opt/minecraft/server.properties
The game's own configuration \(em the other half of the split described above.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mc (1),
.BR mc-backup (1),
.BR mc-plugins (5),
.BR systemd.time (7)
